window.OPENCRAWL_DOCS = {"introduction":["Introduction","Start here","\n<p class=\"doc-lead\">OpenCrawl is an authenticated internet capability gateway for agents. Instead of wiring every AI client directly to dozens of scrapers, browser runtimes, APIs, SaaS accounts and MCP servers, you give the client one stable MCP endpoint and let OpenCrawl handle discovery, policy, credentials, metering and execution behind that boundary.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"why\">Why OpenCrawl exists</h2>\n<p>Agent stacks become difficult to operate when every provider is exposed directly. Tool schemas consume model context, credentials end up scattered across clients, provider-specific failures leak into prompts, and replacing one upstream service forces you to reconfigure every agent. OpenCrawl inserts a control plane between the agent and those providers.</p>\n\n<div class=\"doc-card-grid\">\n<article><span>ONE ENDPOINT</span><h3>Stable client connection</h3><p>Clients connect to one Streamable HTTP MCP endpoint even while providers change behind it.</p><a data-link href=\"/docs/mcp\">Understand MCP →</a></article>\n<article><span>SMALL CONTEXT</span><h3>Discover before loading</h3><p>The agent routes intent and loads only the tool schema it actually needs instead of receiving a giant catalog up front.</p><a data-link href=\"/docs/tool-mesh\">Understand the Tool Mesh →</a></article>\n<article><span>CONTROL</span><h3>Policy before execution</h3><p>Identity, scopes, plan rules, wallet reservations and provider policy are evaluated before work is executed.</p><a data-link href=\"/docs/security\">Read the security model →</a></article>\n<article><span>TRACEABILITY</span><h3>Know what happened</h3><p>Metered executions are correlated with request IDs, provider selection, status, spend and latency.</p><a data-link href=\"/docs/usage\">Read usage & debugging →</a></article>\n</div>\n\n<h2 id=\"model\">The mental model</h2>\n<p>Think of OpenCrawl as a router rather than a giant bag of tools. A client describes an intent. The gateway authenticates the caller. The Tool Mesh searches the available capability fabric. The agent inspects one candidate when necessary, executes it, and receives a normalized result with provenance and usage information.</p>\n\n<pre class=\"doc-diagram\">client / agent\n      ↓\nOpenCrawl /mcp\n      ↓\nauthenticate + authorize\n      ↓\npolicy + wallet reservation\n      ↓\ncapability router / Tool Mesh\n      ↓\nnative web · browser · OpenAPI · remote MCP · connected apps\n      ↓\nnormalized result + request ID + usage ledger</pre>\n\n<h2 id=\"not\">What OpenCrawl is not</h2>\n<p>It is not a promise that every upstream system is always available, and it does not make one provider magically equivalent to another. Providers have different permissions, latency, freshness, rate limits and cost. OpenCrawl normalizes discovery and execution while preserving enough provider/provenance information to understand what actually ran.</p>\n\n<aside class=\"doc-note\"><b>Start with the flow, not the catalog.</b><p>For a new integration, connect one client, run one harmless discovery request, inspect the chosen capability, then expand scopes or providers only when the need is clear. This keeps failures explainable and credentials bounded.</p></aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"next\">Where to go next</h2>\n<p><a data-link href=\"/docs/quickstart\">Quickstart</a> takes you from account creation to the first successful request. <a data-link href=\"/docs/clients\">Client guides</a> cover ChatGPT, Claude Code, VS Code, Cursor and generic MCP clients. <a data-link href=\"/docs/sync\">Connections & synchronization</a> explains how client connections differ from connected SaaS accounts.</p>\n"],"architecture":["Architecture","Start here","\n<p class=\"doc-lead\">OpenCrawl deliberately separates identity, application state, provider credentials and execution. That separation is not cosmetic: authentication should not silently own your billing ledger, and a provider token should never become browser-visible just because an agent can use the provider.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"layers\">System layers</h2>\n<div class=\"doc-stack\">\n<div><b>Client layer</b><p>ChatGPT, Claude Code, VS Code, Cursor, scripts or another MCP host. The client knows the OpenCrawl endpoint and an authorization method; it does not need every upstream credential.</p></div>\n<div><b>Gateway layer</b><p><code>/mcp</code> receives Streamable HTTP MCP traffic. Authentication, scopes, plan rules, rate limits and wallet reservation are enforced at this boundary.</p></div>\n<div><b>Capability layer</b><p>The Tool Mesh and capability packs search native and external providers without loading every provider schema into the model context.</p></div>\n<div><b>Execution layer</b><p>Native web collection, browser/sandbox work, OpenAPI operations, remote MCP servers and configured providers perform the actual work.</p></div>\n<div><b>Control plane</b><p>Neon stores OpenCrawl application state: users, sessions, API keys, wallets, usage, connections, monitors and security records. Supabase Auth remains the external password/identity provider; it does not own OpenCrawl's control ledger.</p></div>\n<div><b>Messaging & payment</b><p>Transactional mail is sent through Resend with configured SMTP fallback. Payment orders are verified server-side before plan or wallet entitlements are fulfilled.</p></div>\n</div>\n\n<h2 id=\"identity-separation\">Why identity and application state are separate</h2>\n<p>An auth provider answers “did this user prove control of these credentials?” OpenCrawl must answer different questions: which OpenCrawl user does that identity map to, what scopes/keys belong to them, whether 2FA is enabled, how much wallet balance exists, what sessions are active, and what was executed. Keeping those responsibilities separate prevents an auth-provider migration from becoming a full product-database migration.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"provider-secrets\">Why provider credentials stay server-side</h2>\n<p>Connected-app tokens, provider API keys and remote MCP credentials can grant real external access. OpenCrawl stores and injects those credentials at the provider boundary so the model sees schemas, account aliases and results rather than raw secrets. Public connection configuration and secret configuration are treated separately.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"failure-domains\">Failure domains</h2>\n<p>A single provider timeout should not make the gateway disappear. Discovery can skip an unhealthy external source, async providers can return job handles, and failed executions remain traceable in the ledger. Authentication, billing and credential failures fail closed because continuing would create a security or accounting ambiguity.</p>\n\n<aside class=\"doc-note important\"><b>Build green ≠ runtime healthy.</b><p>CI proves code and tests. Runtime health additionally depends on database credentials, auth keys, encryption material, mail configuration, payment configuration and provider connectivity. OpenCrawl exposes runtime status separately for that reason.</p></aside>\n"],"quickstart":["Quickstart","Start here","\n<p class=\"doc-lead\">The shortest useful OpenCrawl setup is: verify one account, create one narrow credential, connect one client, discover one capability and execute one request. Do not start by enabling every provider.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"step-1\">1. Create and verify your account</h2>\n<ol class=\"doc-steps\">\n<li><span>01</span><div><b>Create the identity</b><p>Sign up with email/password or an available external identity route. OpenCrawl maps that external identity to an internal Neon user record.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>02</span><div><b>Verify email</b><p>Follow the link or enter the six-digit challenge. Privileged control-plane operations remain blocked until verification succeeds.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>03</span><div><b>Optionally enable 2FA</b><p>TOTP adds a second factor after primary sign-in. Save recovery codes somewhere independent of the authenticator device.</p></div></li>\n</ol>\n<p><a data-link href=\"/docs/account\">Read the full identity flow</a> · <a data-link href=\"/docs/two-factor\">Understand 2FA before enabling it</a></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"step-2\">2. Choose the right credential</h2>\n<p>For a human-operated MCP client that supports compatible OAuth, prefer OAuth: the user sees consent and the client receives an authorization token. For servers, CI, scripts or clients without suitable interactive OAuth, create a scoped OpenCrawl API key.</p>\n\n<div class=\"doc-card-grid\">\n<article><span>INTERACTIVE</span><h3>OAuth MCP</h3><p>Best when a user is present to authorize a client and the host can follow MCP OAuth discovery.</p><a data-link href=\"/docs/oauth\">OAuth guide →</a></article>\n<article><span>AUTOMATION</span><h3>Scoped API key</h3><p>Best for unattended services, CI, scripts or explicit Bearer-header configuration.</p><a data-link href=\"/docs/keys\">Key guide →</a></article>\n</div>\n\n<h2 id=\"step-3\">3. Connect the MCP endpoint</h2>\n<pre><code>{{ORIGIN}}/mcp</code></pre>\n<p>OpenCrawl exposes the customer surface over remote Streamable HTTP. Point a compatible client at that URL and either complete OAuth or supply a dedicated scoped Bearer key.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"step-4\">4. Run the first discovery flow</h2>\n<pre><code>mesh_route(\"fetch a public page and return structured evidence\")\n\nmesh_capability_resolve(\"web.fetch.page\")\n# or, when you intentionally want a provider-specific tool:\nmesh_describe(\"provider:tool\")\n\nmesh_capability_execute(\"web.fetch.page\", {...})</code></pre>\n<p>Route first because context is expensive. The model should not receive thousands of unrelated schemas. It asks what can satisfy the intent, opens the relevant schema, then executes.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"step-5\">5. Inspect what happened</h2>\n<p>Open <a data-link href=\"/dashboard/usage\">Runs & usage</a> and keep the request ID. It is the correlation key for provider selection, failures, latency and settlement. If the provider returns a job handle, poll the job/result APIs instead of launching duplicate work.</p>\n\n<aside class=\"doc-note\"><b>Good first test.</b><p>Use a harmless read-only public-web request. Once discovery, execution, provenance and usage accounting are understandable, connect sensitive accounts or allow side-effecting tools.</p></aside>\n"],"account":["Accounts & verification","Identity & security","\n<p class=\"doc-lead\">OpenCrawl has two identity layers: an external authentication identity and an internal OpenCrawl account. The mapping is intentional. It lets authentication evolve without making the auth provider the owner of wallets, API keys, monitors, usage or security state.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"signup\">Signup</h2>\n<p>Email/password signup creates or links the external Supabase Auth identity, then creates or links the corresponding <code>ih_users</code> record in Neon. OpenCrawl does not need Supabase database tables to own the application profile anymore; the control plane lives in Neon.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"verification\">Email verification</h2>\n<p>Verification is a product security gate, not a decorative badge. Unverified users cannot perform privileged mutations. A verification challenge can be completed by link or six-digit code, expires, is rate-limited, and newer challenges invalidate the earlier unused challenge.</p>\n<p>Transactional messages are recorded in the OpenCrawl email event ledger and sent through the configured mail provider. That lets operators distinguish “an event was created” from “the provider accepted it” and “the provider reports it delivered.”</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"signin\">Sign-in</h2>\n<p>Primary credentials are verified by the identity provider. OpenCrawl then resolves the mapped Neon user. If TOTP is enabled, a normal OpenCrawl session is not issued yet: the server creates a short-lived 2FA challenge and waits for the second factor.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"github\">GitHub sign-in</h2>\n<p>GitHub OAuth can identify a user and link GitHub metadata, but it does not bypass OpenCrawl verification or 2FA. Product security gates are applied after the external identity step.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"sessions\">Sessions</h2>\n<p>Web sessions are opaque random values stored in HTTP-only cookies; only token hashes persist in Neon. Password change/reset revokes active sessions so a stolen session does not silently survive a credential reset.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"recovery\">Password recovery</h2>\n<p>Password reset links are time-limited and one-use. The reset updates the external password where applicable and revokes existing OpenCrawl sessions. The mail delivery path remains separately auditable through the email event ledger.</p>\n\n<aside class=\"doc-note important\"><b>Three systems, three jobs.</b><p>Supabase Auth proves credentials. Neon owns OpenCrawl application/security state. Resend transports security email. Diagnose the failing layer instead of copying identity state between all three.</p></aside>\n"],"two-factor":["Two-factor authentication","Identity & security","\n<p class=\"doc-lead\">OpenCrawl uses time-based one-time passwords (TOTP) as an optional second factor. The TOTP secret is encrypted at rest, recovery codes are hashed and one-use, and successful authentication counters are tracked to reject replay.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"setup\">Enable TOTP</h2>\n<ol class=\"doc-steps\">\n<li><span>01</span><div><b>Start setup</b><p>Open Settings → Security → Set up. OpenCrawl generates a fresh pending secret.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>02</span><div><b>Scan or enter the secret</b><p>Scan the QR code with an authenticator app or enter the manual key. The QR contains an <code>otpauth://</code> URI for OpenCrawl, the account email, SHA-1, six digits and a 30-second period.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>03</span><div><b>Confirm possession</b><p>Enter the current six-digit code. This verifies that the authenticator received the same secret. The setup verification code is not consumed as the first real login replay counter.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>04</span><div><b>Store recovery codes</b><p>OpenCrawl shows one-use recovery codes and stores only their hashes. Save them somewhere independent of the authenticator device.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>05</span><div><b>Finish</b><p>Once recovery codes are acknowledged, Settings shows 2FA enabled.</p></div></li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2 id=\"signin\">What changes at sign-in</h2>\n<p>After the password or external identity succeeds, OpenCrawl checks the Neon security record. If TOTP is enabled it creates a short-lived login challenge and does <strong>not</strong> issue the normal session. The authenticator or one unused recovery code completes that challenge; only then is the normal session created.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"replay\">Replay protection</h2>\n<p>A TOTP code is based on the shared secret and a 30-second counter. OpenCrawl allows a small bounded window for reasonable clock drift, then stores the counter used by a real authentication event. An equal or older accepted counter is rejected. This blocks simple replay of a captured code.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"recovery-codes\">Recovery codes</h2>\n<p>A recovery code substitutes for one second-factor challenge. It is normalized before hashing and marked used atomically. Regenerating recovery codes invalidates the previous set.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"disable\">Disable or regenerate</h2>\n<p>Disabling 2FA requires a valid current authenticator or recovery code and removes the encrypted secret plus recovery codes. Regenerating recovery codes requires a valid factor but keeps the TOTP secret.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"failures\">If valid codes keep failing</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Enable automatic time on the authenticator device. TOTP is time-based.</li>\n<li>Wait for a fresh 30-second code if the current code is nearly expired.</li>\n<li>Make sure you scanned the latest pending OpenCrawl setup rather than an older abandoned QR.</li>\n<li>If the API returns <code>two_factor_unavailable</code>, the backend cannot safely read the encrypted TOTP state. That is not a user-code error, and OpenCrawl does not burn challenge attempts in this condition.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<aside class=\"doc-note important\"><b>Encryption-key stability matters.</b><p>The TOTP encryption key must remain stable across deployments. Rotating it without a re-encryption migration makes old encrypted secrets unreadable. OpenCrawl fails closed rather than pretending those users simply typed the wrong code.</p></aside>\n"],"keys":["API keys & scopes","Identity & security","\n<p class=\"doc-lead\">API keys are long-lived machine credentials. Treat them like passwords for software: create the narrowest key that works, separate clients/environments, and rotate one compromised key instead of sharing a universal secret.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"when\">When to use a key</h2>\n<p>Use a key for CI, backend services, cron jobs, unattended agents or clients where interactive MCP OAuth is unavailable or undesirable. For a human-operated client with compatible OAuth, OAuth usually gives a better consent and token lifecycle.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"create\">Creation</h2>\n<p>Open <a data-link href=\"/dashboard/api-keys\">API keys</a>, choose the environment and scopes, then create the key. The raw value is shown once. OpenCrawl stores only its hash, so the original cannot be recovered later.</p>\n<pre><code>Authorization: Bearer ih_live_…\n\n# Some direct HTTP surfaces may also accept:\nX-API-Key: ih_live_…</code></pre>\n\n<h2 id=\"scopes\">Scopes</h2>\n<p>Scopes are one permission boundary, not the only boundary. A call must satisfy key scope, account state, plan policy and provider requirements. Granting a broad scope does not bypass server-side policy.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"isolation\">Isolation pattern</h2>\n<div class=\"doc-card-grid\">\n<article><span>CLIENT</span><h3>One key per client</h3><p>VS Code, CI and a production service should not all share one credential.</p></article>\n<article><span>ENVIRONMENT</span><h3>Separate test/live</h3><p>Do not reuse production secrets in local experiments.</p></article>\n<article><span>SCOPE</span><h3>Least privilege</h3><p>Read-only research does not need mutation permissions.</p></article>\n<article><span>ROTATION</span><h3>Replace, then revoke</h3><p>Deploy a new key, verify traffic moved, then revoke the old key.</p></article>\n</div>\n\n<h2 id=\"storage\">Where keys should live</h2>\n<p>Do not paste keys into prompts, screenshots, public issues or committed configuration. Use environment variables, secret managers or the client's secret-input facility. VS Code, for example, supports input variables so API keys do not need to be hardcoded in <code>mcp.json</code>.</p>\n"],"mcp":["MCP gateway","MCP","\n<p class=\"doc-lead\">Model Context Protocol (MCP) is an open standard for connecting AI hosts to external tools and context. OpenCrawl exposes its customer gateway as a remote Streamable HTTP MCP server.</p>\n<pre><code>{{ORIGIN}}/mcp</code></pre>\n\n<h2 id=\"why\">Why MCP here</h2>\n<p>The value is not merely another API syntax. An MCP-aware client can discover and invoke tools through a common protocol. OpenCrawl then adds routing, policy, provider abstraction, account-aware execution and metering behind that protocol surface.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"transport\">Transport</h2>\n<p>OpenCrawl uses remote Streamable HTTP rather than a local <code>stdio</code> child process. Clients connect over HTTPS. The customer MCP surface is configured stateless at the MCP transport layer, while account/session/usage state remains in OpenCrawl's own control plane.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"primitives\">Protocol primitives vs OpenCrawl</h2>\n<p>MCP can expose tools, resources, prompts and other capabilities. OpenCrawl's customer surface is currently tool-oriented: discovery, capability resolution, execution, monitoring and related actions are the primary exposed contract. Do not assume every primitive in the broader MCP specification is implemented by every OpenCrawl deployment.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"flow\">Recommended discovery flow</h2>\n<pre><code>mesh_route(intent)\n  → mesh_capability_resolve(capability)\n  → mesh_capability_execute(capability, arguments)\n\n# Provider-specific path:\nmesh_search(query)\n  → mesh_describe(ref)\n  → mesh_execute(ref, arguments)</code></pre>\n<p>The two-stage design keeps model context small. Large provider catalogs remain outside the prompt until the agent actually needs one schema.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"references\">Official references</h2>\n<p><a class=\"doc-source-link\" href=\"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Model Context Protocol documentation ↗</a> <a class=\"doc-source-link\" href=\"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/mcp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Anthropic MCP overview ↗</a></p>\n\n<aside class=\"doc-note\"><b>MCP standardization is not a trust certificate.</b><p>A remote MCP server can expose actions with real external effects. Only connect or federate servers you trust, and keep client approval/confirmation for consequential actions where the host supports it.</p></aside>\n"],"oauth":["OAuth for MCP","MCP","\n<p class=\"doc-lead\">OAuth is the interactive connection path. A compatible MCP client discovers OpenCrawl authorization, sends the user through consent, and receives a token representing the approved client/scopes instead of asking the user to paste a permanent OpenCrawl key into that client.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"discovery\">Discovery endpoints</h2>\n<pre><code>GET {{ORIGIN}}/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource\nGET {{ORIGIN}}/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server</code></pre>\n<p>Protected-resource metadata tells the client which authorization server protects <code>/mcp</code>. Authorization-server metadata tells it where the authorization/token endpoints live and what flow is supported.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"flow\">Authorization code + PKCE</h2>\n<ol class=\"doc-steps\">\n<li><span>01</span><div><b>Discover</b><p>The client reads the well-known metadata instead of hardcoding OpenCrawl internals.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>02</span><div><b>Create PKCE material</b><p>The client generates a verifier and sends its S256 challenge with the authorization request.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>03</span><div><b>Authenticate and consent</b><p>The browser identifies the OpenCrawl user and authorizes the requested scopes.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>04</span><div><b>Exchange the code</b><p>The short-lived authorization code is redeemed with the original verifier.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>05</span><div><b>Call MCP</b><p>The client attaches the resulting Bearer authorization to subsequent MCP requests.</p></div></li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2 id=\"pkce\">Why PKCE</h2>\n<p>PKCE binds the authorization code to the client instance that started the flow. Stealing the code alone is not enough to redeem it without the verifier.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"compare\">OAuth vs API key</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>OAuth</th><th>API key</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Best for</td><td>Interactive user clients</td><td>Automation / explicit server config</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Consent</td><td>Browser authorization</td><td>Granted at key creation/configuration</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Credential style</td><td>Token-oriented</td><td>Long-lived until revoked</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Storage</td><td>Client-managed auth token</td><td>Operator/client secret storage</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n<h2 id=\"debug\">If OAuth loops or returns 401</h2>\n<p>Verify the exact <code>/mcp</code> URL and HTTPS first. A 401 before authorization can be expected for a protected resource. Repeated 401 after consent usually means an expired token, wrong resource/audience, missing scope or a client that did not preserve the authorization state.</p>\n"],"clients":["Client setup guides","MCP","\n<p class=\"doc-lead\">MCP is standardized; host configuration is not. ChatGPT, Codex, Claude Code, VS Code and Cursor all expose remote MCP differently. The OpenCrawl side remains stable: <code>{{ORIGIN}}/mcp</code> over Streamable HTTP, authenticated by OAuth when compatible or by a dedicated scoped Bearer key.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"chatgpt\">ChatGPT</h2>\n<p>ChatGPT custom MCP support is exposed through Apps and developer mode. OpenAI currently documents full MCP, including write/modify actions, for Business and Enterprise/Edu workspaces, while Pro developer-mode support is more limited; availability and permissions can change, so use the linked OpenAI page as the current source of truth. Custom MCP apps are configured on ChatGPT web, and workspace admins can restrict developer mode, actions and access.</p><p>In a supported workspace, create a custom app, enter the OpenCrawl remote MCP endpoint, choose the available authentication mechanism, run <strong>Scan Tools</strong>, complete OAuth if prompted, and review the discovered actions before enabling the app. New or changed write actions should be treated as a permission change rather than automatically trusted.</p>\n<ol class=\"doc-steps\">\n<li><span>01</span><div><b>Open Apps / developer settings</b><p>Use the custom-app or developer-mode creation flow available to your account/workspace.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>02</span><div><b>Enter OpenCrawl</b><p>Use <code>{{ORIGIN}}/mcp</code> as the remote MCP endpoint.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>03</span><div><b>Authenticate</b><p>Complete OpenCrawl OAuth when offered. If your supported workflow uses a static credential, create a dedicated OpenCrawl key instead of reusing another client's key.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>04</span><div><b>Scan and review tools</b><p>Treat newly discovered or changed write actions as a permission change, not just a refresh.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>05</span><div><b>Test read-only first</b><p>Run a harmless public-data action and verify it appears in OpenCrawl usage before relying on consequential actions.</p></div></li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><a class=\"doc-source-link\" href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/12584461-developer-mode-and-mcp-apps-in-chatgpt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OpenAI: Developer mode and MCP apps in ChatGPT ↗</a></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"codex\">Codex CLI / IDE extension</h2>\n<p>OpenAI documents remote MCP setup in Codex with <code>codex mcp add &lt;name&gt; --url &lt;server&gt;</code>. Codex CLI and the IDE extension share MCP configuration, so you normally register the server once and verify it with <code>codex mcp list</code>.</p>\n<pre><code>codex mcp add opencrawl --url {{ORIGIN}}/mcp\ncodex mcp list</code></pre>\n<p>For a manual configuration, Codex MCP servers can be represented in <code>~/.codex/config.toml</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>[mcp_servers.opencrawl]\nurl = \"{{ORIGIN}}/mcp\"</code></pre>\n<p>The command/config only tells Codex where OpenCrawl lives. Authentication is still a separate concern: use the client-supported authorization flow or a properly scoped credential, and do not copy a permanent secret into <code>AGENTS.md</code>, source code or prompts.</p>\n<p><a class=\"doc-source-link\" href=\"https://developers.openai.com/learn/docs-mcp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OpenAI Developers: MCP server setup for Codex and editors ↗</a></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"claude-code\">Claude Code</h2>\n<p>Claude Code supports remote HTTP MCP servers directly. Add OpenCrawl with the HTTP transport, then use the MCP management view to inspect or authenticate the server.</p>\n<pre><code>claude mcp add --transport http opencrawl {{ORIGIN}}/mcp\n\n# Then inside Claude Code:\n/mcp</code></pre>\n<p>If the server advertises OAuth, Claude Code can drive the browser authorization flow. For a key-based connection, use Claude's supported header/secret configuration rather than placing the key in a prompt.</p>\n<p><a class=\"doc-source-link\" href=\"https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code/mcp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Anthropic: Connect Claude Code to tools with MCP ↗</a></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"vscode\">VS Code / GitHub Copilot</h2>\n<p>VS Code supports remote HTTP MCP servers in <code>.vscode/mcp.json</code> and portable <code>.mcp.json</code> configurations. For a Bearer-key connection, use an input variable so the secret is prompted/stored separately instead of committed to the workspace.</p>\n<pre><code>{\n  \"inputs\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"promptString\",\n      \"id\": \"opencrawl-key\",\n      \"description\": \"OpenCrawl API key\",\n      \"password\": true\n    }\n  ],\n  \"servers\": {\n    \"opencrawl\": {\n      \"type\": \"http\",\n      \"url\": \"{{ORIGIN}}/mcp\",\n      \"headers\": {\n        \"Authorization\": \"Bearer ${input:opencrawl-key}\"\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>VS Code also supports OAuth configuration for HTTP MCP servers. Choose OAuth when you want interactive consent; use a protected input-variable key when you want deterministic key-based automation.</p>\n<p><a class=\"doc-source-link\" href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">VS Code: Add and manage MCP servers ↗</a> <a class=\"doc-source-link\" href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agents/reference/mcp-configuration\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">VS Code MCP configuration reference ↗</a></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"cursor\">Cursor</h2>\n<p>Cursor supports remote Streamable HTTP MCP servers and project/global configuration. Project-specific configuration belongs in <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code>. Never commit a real OpenCrawl key into a shared repository.</p>\n<pre><code>{\n  \"mcpServers\": {\n    \"opencrawl\": {\n      \"url\": \"{{ORIGIN}}/mcp\"\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>When authentication is required, use Cursor's supported OAuth or credential/environment configuration rather than embedding secrets in source control.</p>\n<p><a class=\"doc-source-link\" href=\"https://docs.cursor.com/context/model-context-protocol\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Cursor: Model Context Protocol ↗</a></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"generic\">Generic MCP client</h2>\n<p>A generic client needs Streamable HTTP support, the endpoint and an auth method. If it implements compatible MCP OAuth discovery, point it at the endpoint and follow discovery. Otherwise configure a dedicated scoped key as an Authorization Bearer header.</p>\n<pre><code>Endpoint: {{ORIGIN}}/mcp\nTransport: Streamable HTTP\nAuthorization: OAuth discovery\n\n# or\nAuthorization: Bearer ih_live_…</code></pre>\n\n<aside class=\"doc-note\"><b>Do not copy one host's configuration blindly.</b><p>Protocol calls can be portable while config filenames, OAuth UX, secret storage and tool-approval controls remain client-specific.</p></aside>\n"],"sync":["Connections & synchronization","MCP","\n<p class=\"doc-lead\">“Connect OpenCrawl to Claude” and “connect OpenCrawl to Gmail, Notion or GitHub” are different operations. The first connects an MCP <strong>client to OpenCrawl</strong>. The second connects an <strong>upstream account/provider to OpenCrawl</strong>. Mixing those layers is how permission boundaries become unclear.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"client-sync\">Client connection</h2>\n<p>Multiple MCP clients can point at the same OpenCrawl account and therefore see the account's server-side capabilities and policy. Each client should still have its own OAuth grant or API key so one client can be revoked without breaking the others.</p>\n<pre class=\"doc-diagram\">ChatGPT ─┐\nClaude ──┼──→ OpenCrawl /mcp ──→ one account policy / Tool Mesh\nVS Code ─┤\nCursor ──┘\n\nEach client: separate OAuth grant or scoped key</pre>\n\n<h2 id=\"connected-apps\">Connected applications</h2>\n<p>Connected SaaS accounts are upstream capabilities. When the Composio bridge is configured, OpenCrawl can discover account-aware tools and execute them while authorization material stays server-side. The model receives a tool reference/account alias, not the raw provider token.</p>\n<p>This is not bulk data replication. Connecting an app does not mean OpenCrawl continuously copies the service. A tool call requests a specific operation; OpenCrawl selects the configured account, sends the minimum needed input to the provider/bridge, and returns the result.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"platform-examples\">What “sync” means for common platforms</h2>\n<p>When the corresponding toolkit and connected account are configured, OpenCrawl can expose account-aware actions through the Tool Mesh. “Connected” means the agent can make authorized tool calls on demand; it does <strong>not</strong> mean OpenCrawl continuously clones the provider into Neon.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Platform type</th><th>What a connection can enable</th><th>What it does not imply</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>GitHub</td><td>Repository, issue, pull-request or other toolkit actions permitted by the connected account and selected tool.</td><td>No automatic mirror of every repository or permission to every organization.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gmail / email</td><td>Mailbox operations exposed by the configured toolkit for the authorized account.</td><td>No continuous inbox copy and no authority to act as another connected mailbox.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Slack / messaging</td><td>Channel, message or workspace operations permitted by the provider scopes and selected account.</td><td>No background channel synchronization unless a separate workflow/monitor explicitly performs it.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Notion / knowledge tools</td><td>Page, database or search operations exposed by the connected toolkit.</td><td>No automatic clone of the entire workspace into OpenCrawl.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Drive / calendar / productivity</td><td>Explicit file, event or account actions supported by the connected toolkit.</td><td>No cross-account access merely because another account for the same toolkit is connected.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Composio documents connected accounts as per-user authorized connections to a toolkit. Managed connections keep provider credentials outside normal tool arguments and can require re-authorization if the upstream grant expires. OpenCrawl layers its own account allow-list and explicit account selection on top of that provider model.</p>\n<p><a class=\"doc-source-link\" href=\"https://docs.composio.dev/docs/authentication\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Composio: authentication and connected accounts ↗</a> <a class=\"doc-source-link\" href=\"https://docs.composio.dev/reference/v3/api-reference/connected-accounts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Composio connected-account lifecycle ↗</a></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"multiple-accounts\">Multiple accounts</h2>\n<p>If more than one allowed account is active for a toolkit, OpenCrawl requires explicit account selection rather than silently choosing one. This prevents actions such as sending from the wrong mailbox or writing to the wrong repository/workspace.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"remote-mcp\">Remote MCP federation</h2>\n<p>Configured remote MCP servers can join the same Tool Mesh. Their tools receive provider-qualified references and become discoverable alongside native capabilities. The remote server remains its own trust boundary; federation does not certify it as safe.</p>\n<p>The current manual outbound-connection form supports no-auth, API-key, Bearer, custom-header and an <strong>already-issued OAuth access token</strong>. That OAuth field is not an automatic provider login or refresh-token system: if the remote server requires an interactive OAuth lifecycle, obtain/manage that grant through a supported connection flow rather than assuming the manual token field will renew itself.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"openapi\">OpenAPI catalogs</h2>\n<p>Configured public OpenAPI documents can become normalized operations. This is useful when a service has a machine-readable API but no MCP server. Readiness, authentication and side-effect metadata still matter at execution time.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"pattern\">A scalable connection pattern</h2>\n<ol class=\"doc-steps\">\n<li><span>01</span><div><b>Connect OpenCrawl once per client</b><p>Use OAuth or a dedicated key for ChatGPT, Claude, VS Code, CI and other hosts.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>02</span><div><b>Connect upstream accounts server-side</b><p>Use supported connected-account/provider paths. Never paste OAuth tokens into model prompts.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>03</span><div><b>Discover at runtime</b><p>Let the Tool Mesh surface the provider/account-aware tool only when the intent needs it.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>04</span><div><b>Confirm side effects</b><p>Messages, deployments, writes, purchases and destructive operations should remain explicit and reviewable.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>05</span><div><b>Audit by request ID</b><p>Use OpenCrawl usage plus the upstream provider's own activity/audit history when investigating an action.</p></div></li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2 id=\"after\">What happens after connection</h2>\n<p>A connection makes a provider eligible; it does not make the model omniscient. The agent still needs the right capability, valid arguments, sufficient OpenCrawl scopes/plan access and sufficient upstream permissions.</p>\n"],"capabilities":["Capability fabric","Platform","\n<p class=\"doc-lead\">A capability is the user-facing outcome OpenCrawl can provide; a provider is one possible implementation. Keeping those concepts separate lets the same semantic task route to native code, a remote MCP server, an OpenAPI operation or another provider without forcing agents to memorize vendor-specific names.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"web\">Web intelligence</h2>\n<p>Native and configured providers can search, fetch, extract structured content, inspect machine-readable interfaces and perform bounded crawls. Public-target validation and provenance are part of the contract; public-web research must not silently become access to private infrastructure.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"browser\">Browser & sandbox</h2>\n<p>Dynamic sites may need a browser rather than plain HTTP. Browser and sandbox capabilities run in isolated execution paths with bounded runtime and network restrictions. Use them when interaction/rendering is necessary, not as the default for every request.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"apis\">APIs and catalogs</h2>\n<p>OpenAPI descriptions, provider manifests and curated integrations can become structured operations. Discovery should expose whether the provider is ready and what authentication, plan or side-effect constraints apply before execution.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"federation\">Remote MCP federation</h2>\n<p>Configured remote MCP servers can be searched alongside native providers. OpenCrawl normalizes the reference/metadata; the remote server still owns its behavior, data and uptime.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"connected\">Connected apps</h2>\n<p>Connected-account providers add account-aware actions such as repository, messaging or productivity operations. Sensitive provider authorization remains server-side and outside normal model-visible arguments.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"specialized\">Specialized public intelligence</h2>\n<p>OpenCrawl can expose domain-specific packs, such as supported gaming profile/progression paths or phone/public-identity research, through the same routing and metering model. These capabilities still depend on real upstream public/provider data. If the required upstream data is unavailable or private, the correct behavior is to report that limitation rather than fabricate a result.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"stable-v-raw\">Stable capability vs raw provider tool</h2>\n<p>Use a stable capability when you care about the outcome and can tolerate provider fallback. Use a raw provider reference when provider-specific behavior, fields or side effects matter. Stable capabilities improve portability; raw refs improve control.</p>\n"],"tool-mesh":["Tool Mesh","Platform","\n<p class=\"doc-lead\">The Tool Mesh is the discovery/execution layer that keeps large provider catalogs out of the prompt until they are relevant.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"route\">mesh_route</h2>\n<p><code>mesh_route(intent)</code> searches semantic capability packs and external catalogs together. Give it the goal, not a guessed vendor. It returns a shortlist rather than executing automatically.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"resolve\">Resolve or describe</h2>\n<p>For a stable semantic capability, use <code>mesh_capability_resolve</code>. For a provider-specific reference, use <code>mesh_describe</code> or <code>mesh_describe_many</code>. These return normalized schema/metadata so the agent can construct a valid call and understand readiness or side effects.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"execute\">Execute</h2>\n<p><code>mesh_capability_execute</code> runs a stable capability with its configured candidate/fallback logic. <code>mesh_execute</code> calls one explicit provider reference. When supported, <code>dry_run</code> resolves the intended provider and arguments without performing the external action.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"async\">Asynchronous providers</h2>\n<p>Some providers cannot finish within one request. They return a job or result handle. Continue with <code>mesh_job_status</code> and <code>mesh_results</code>; do not repeatedly start duplicate jobs because the original is still working.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"batch\">Batch execution</h2>\n<p>Batch execution is for independent calls and is bounded by concurrency and batch-size controls. A batch is not a database transaction: one provider call can fail while another succeeds. Dependent workflows should execute step-by-step.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"side-effects\">Side effects</h2>\n<p>OpenCrawl preserves read-only or side-effect metadata where known. Missing read-only metadata is not treated as proof of safety. For connected apps and mutation tools, describe/dry-run first where practical and preserve client confirmation for consequential actions.</p>\n\n<aside class=\"doc-note\"><b>Why this is two-stage.</b><p>Routing/search first and schema loading second keeps context focused. Dumping hundreds of unrelated tools into the model at once makes tool choice less reliable and wastes tokens.</p></aside>\n"],"providers":["Providers & upstreams","Platform","\n<p class=\"doc-lead\">Providers are replaceable execution backends. “Configured” does not mean every operation is always available. Discovery should expose readiness and execution should return real upstream failures instead of inventing successful data.</p>\n\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Provider class</th><th>Use</th><th>Credential model</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Native web</td><td>First-party public fetch/search/extraction</td><td>Server/runtime policy; usually no user upstream token for public data</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Browser / sandbox</td><td>Dynamic interaction and isolated execution</td><td>Server-side infrastructure authorization</td></tr>\n<tr><td>OpenAPI</td><td>Operations imported from configured API documents</td><td>Depends on source/provider</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Remote MCP</td><td>Federated tools from another MCP server</td><td>None, API key, Bearer, custom headers or OAuth-derived headers</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Composio bridge</td><td>Account-aware SaaS tools</td><td>Connected accounts selected server-side</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Firecrawl / Apify / similar</td><td>Specialized crawl, scrape or actor workloads</td><td>Provider credentials injected server-side</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Curated data providers</td><td>Specialized structured data</td><td>Provider-specific</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n<h2 id=\"selection\">Selection and fallback</h2>\n<p>Capability packs can rank candidate providers. Readiness, plan policy, side-effect classification and cost rules are evaluated before or around execution. A fallback must preserve the semantic goal; it must not pretend provider-specific fields exist when they do not.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"failure\">What a provider failure means</h2>\n<p>An upstream timeout, authentication failure, rate limit or schema change is not the same as an OpenCrawl authentication failure. Keep the request ID, provider reference, tool name and upstream status when debugging so the failing boundary is obvious.</p>\n"],"monitoring":["Monitoring","Operations","\n<p class=\"doc-lead\">Monitors turn a point-in-time capability into a repeated observation. They are for detecting changes or failures over time, not for continuously hammering an endpoint or replacing a full observability platform.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"types\">Monitor types</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Web page:</strong> HTTP/content checks on a public page.</li>\n<li><strong>API endpoint:</strong> status or structured JSON-oriented checks.</li>\n<li><strong>MCP endpoint:</strong> reachability/behavior checks for an MCP target.</li>\n<li><strong>Gaming identity:</strong> supported public-profile checks where a capability exists.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"lifecycle\">Lifecycle</h2>\n<ol class=\"doc-steps\">\n<li><span>01</span><div><b>Create</b><p>Choose type, target and interval.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>02</span><div><b>Validate</b><p>OpenCrawl applies target and policy validation before scheduling.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>03</span><div><b>Run</b><p>The monitor executor performs bounded checks when due.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>04</span><div><b>Record</b><p>Status, timing and relevant result data are stored in history.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>05</span><div><b>Act</b><p>Use history to diagnose incidents or feed a higher-level notification/automation flow.</p></div></li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2 id=\"intervals\">Choosing an interval</h2>\n<p>Use the slowest interval that still matters operationally. A daily content check does not need a 15-minute schedule. Frequent polling consumes provider capacity, may cost wallet balance and can hit upstream limits.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"limits\">What a green check proves</h2>\n<p>A successful monitor proves that defined probe succeeded at that time. It does not prove every geography, authenticated path or downstream dependency is healthy. Use multiple narrow monitors when the system has distinct failure domains.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"btn small\" data-link href=\"/dashboard/monitors\">Open monitors</a></p>\n"],"usage":["Usage, wallet & request IDs","Operations","\n<p class=\"doc-lead\">OpenCrawl meters work through an internal integer ledger but presents the user-facing service balance in USD. Internal units exist for exact reservation/settlement math; users should reason about the dollar-denominated wallet shown in the product.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"reservation\">Reservation and settlement</h2>\n<p>Before metered work begins, OpenCrawl can reserve enough balance for the bounded operation. After execution, measured usage settles the actual charge and releases unused reservation. This prevents concurrent expensive calls from all spending the same available balance.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"buckets\">Monthly vs purchased balance</h2>\n<p>Monthly plan balance refreshes with the plan period. Purchased balance is rollover service capacity. The wallet shows monthly, purchased and reserved amounts separately so in-flight work is not mistaken for spendable balance.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"request-id\">Request IDs</h2>\n<p>Every important metered execution should be traceable by request ID. When reporting a problem, include that ID instead of only saying “the tool failed.” It lets you correlate provider choice, status, latency, settlement and result metadata without exposing credentials.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"runs\">Reading Runs & usage</h2>\n<p>The usage surface answers four questions: what was called, which provider handled it, whether it completed, and how much service balance it consumed. Provider-specific pricing can differ, so visually similar operations are not guaranteed to cost the same.</p>\n\n<aside class=\"doc-note\"><b>Internal units are not a second currency.</b><p>The integer ledger is an implementation detail for exact accounting. The product-facing balance is USD-denominated service capacity; users should not have to reason in raw credits.</p></aside>\n"],"billing":["Billing & top-ups","Commercial","\n<p class=\"doc-lead\">Billing is server-authoritative. The browser can request a plan or wallet top-up, but it cannot decide how much entitlement should be granted.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"flow\">Checkout flow</h2>\n<ol class=\"doc-steps\">\n<li><span>01</span><div><b>User selects an amount or plan</b><p>The browser sends intent, not a trusted entitlement.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>02</span><div><b>Server creates the payment order</b><p>Amount and currency are calculated or validated server-side.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>03</span><div><b>Provider captures payment</b><p>The hosted checkout handles the payment details.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>04</span><div><b>OpenCrawl verifies</b><p>Order ID, captured state, exact amount and currency must match the server record.</p></div></li>\n<li><span>05</span><div><b>Fulfillment occurs once</b><p>The verified payment updates plan or purchased wallet state idempotently.</p></div></li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2 id=\"manual\">Manual USD top-up</h2>\n<p>Custom wallet top-ups accept a bounded USD amount with cent precision. The server derives internal units from that amount and verifies that the captured payment matches the stored USD cents before granting purchased balance. The browser never supplies trusted wallet units.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"preset\">Preset packs</h2>\n<p>Preset packs can have their own transaction currency and price. Payment history therefore displays the actual transaction currency, while resulting OpenCrawl service capacity is displayed in the wallet's USD denomination.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"idempotency\">Why repeated webhooks do not double-credit</h2>\n<p>Fulfillment is tied to stored payment/order state. Reprocessing the same captured event resolves to the existing fulfillment rather than inserting a second wallet grant.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"mismatch\">If payment is captured but balance does not change</h2>\n<p>Do not manually add balance first. Compare the order/payment reference, captured state, amount/currency verification and fulfillment ledger. Manual correction without understanding the failed boundary creates reconciliation debt.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"btn small\" data-link href=\"/dashboard/billing\">Open billing</a> <a class=\"btn small\" data-link href=\"/dashboard/wallet\">Open wallet</a></p>\n"],"security":["Security model","Identity & security","\n<p class=\"doc-lead\">OpenCrawl security is layered: verify identity, scope credentials, keep upstream secrets server-side, restrict network targets, distinguish reads from mutations, meter bounded work and keep correlation/audit records.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"credentials\">Credential handling</h2>\n<p>Customer API keys are stored as hashes. TOTP secrets are encrypted. Recovery codes are hashed. Web sessions use HTTP-only cookies and persist as token hashes. Provider and connected-account credentials belong server-side rather than in browser-visible configuration or model prompts.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"network\">Network boundary</h2>\n<p>Public-web execution validates target URLs and rejects localhost, private, link-local, multicast, reserved and metadata-network targets. Redirects and browser subrequests need equivalent checks because validating only the first URL is not sufficient protection against SSRF.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"mcp-trust\">MCP trust</h2>\n<p>MCP standardizes communication; it does not certify a server as safe. A remote MCP can expose tools that read data or perform writes. Only federate servers you trust, preserve tool metadata, and keep user/client confirmation for consequential actions.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"injection\">Prompt injection</h2>\n<p>Web pages, emails, documents and remote-tool outputs are untrusted data. Retrieved text can contain instructions attempting to redirect the agent. Treat provider content as data, keep system/operator policy separate, and require explicit authorization for consequential actions.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"side-effects\">Side effects</h2>\n<p>Writes should be explicit. Tool metadata can classify known side effects, but missing read-only metadata is not proof of safety. For ambiguous tools, describe or dry-run first where practical and retain client confirmation.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"twofactor\">2FA and machine credentials</h2>\n<p>TOTP protects web sign-in. API keys and OAuth grants have their own lifecycle. Enabling 2FA does not rotate already-issued machine credentials; revoke or rotate those separately if compromise is suspected.</p>\n\n<p><a data-link href=\"/docs/two-factor\">Read the 2FA lifecycle</a> · <a data-link href=\"/docs/keys\">Read API key isolation</a></p>\n"],"errors":["Errors & limits","Reference","\n<p class=\"doc-lead\">An error should identify the boundary that failed. Authentication, policy, provider, payment and capacity failures should not all collapse into a generic “tool failed” message.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"common\">Common error boundaries</h2>\n<p>Use the error code together with the HTTP status and request ID. The code identifies OpenCrawl's interpretation of the failure; an upstream provider may additionally return its own status or message.</p>\n\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Code / status</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>unauthorized</code> / 401</td><td>No valid OpenCrawl session, token or key</td><td>Re-authenticate or replace/re-authorize the credential.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>email_verification_required</code> / 403</td><td>Identity exists but verification is incomplete</td><td>Complete the current email challenge.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>scope_denied</code> / 403</td><td>Credential lacks a permission</td><td>Grant only the minimum required scope.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>plan_restricted</code> / 403</td><td>Current plan does not allow the operation</td><td>Choose an allowed capability or change plan deliberately.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>insufficient_credits</code></td><td>Wallet cannot fund the reservation</td><td>Wait for refresh or add purchased balance.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>rate_limited</code> / 429</td><td>Policy or upstream cadence limit reached</td><td>Back off; do not retry in a tight loop.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>two_factor_unavailable</code> / 503</td><td>Server cannot safely verify encrypted TOTP state</td><td>Repair backend configuration; do not waste recovery codes.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Provider 4xx/5xx</td><td>Selected upstream rejected or failed the call</td><td>Keep request ID/provider ref and inspect auth, args, limits and provider status.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n<h2 id=\"retries\">Retry discipline</h2>\n<p>Retry network/transient failures with bounded backoff. Do not retry validation, scope or payment-mismatch errors unchanged. For side-effecting tools, determine whether the upstream action occurred before retrying; otherwise a timeout can become a duplicate action.</p>\n"],"troubleshooting":["Troubleshooting","Reference","\n<p class=\"doc-lead\">Debug from the boundary inward. Start with the exact route, request ID and status rather than changing several services at once.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"mcp-401\">MCP returns 401</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Confirm the URL ends in <code>/mcp</code> and uses HTTPS.</li>\n<li>For OAuth, inspect protected-resource discovery and repeat authorization.</li>\n<li>For a key, confirm it is active, belongs to the intended environment and is actually sent as a Bearer header.</li>\n<li>Never paste the key into chat to test it.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2 id=\"no-tools\">Client connects but shows no tools</h2>\n<p>Refresh or rescan the MCP server in the client, verify the remote HTTP transport, then inspect provider/capability readiness. Some hosts cache tool lists until a refresh or restart.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"email\">Security email does not arrive</h2>\n<p>Separate event creation from delivery. Check whether OpenCrawl created the email event, whether the provider accepted it, and whether the provider reports delivered or bounced. Repeatedly clicking resend can hit cooldown and does not repair sender-domain problems.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"totp\">Authenticator code is rejected</h2>\n<p>Enable automatic device time, wait for a fresh code, and verify you scanned the latest pending setup. A <code>two_factor_unavailable</code> response is a backend encryption/configuration problem, not a wrong code.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"provider\">A provider tool fails</h2>\n<p>Capture the request ID, provider reference, tool name, status and latency. Check provider readiness, account alias requirements, side-effect classification and whether the upstream returned a job handle instead of a final result.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"billing\">Payment captured but wallet unchanged</h2>\n<p>Inspect the stored payment and fulfillment ledger. Confirm order ID, amount, currency and captured state. Do not manually grant balance until you know whether fulfillment was skipped, rejected or delayed.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"client-config\">One client's config does not work in another</h2>\n<p>MCP is portable; client configuration syntax is not. Claude Code CLI flags, VS Code <code>mcp.json</code>, Cursor <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code> and ChatGPT Apps are different setup surfaces. Use the specific guide.</p>\n\n<p><a data-link href=\"/docs/clients\">Open client setup guides</a></p>\n"],"datasets":["Saved datasets","Collect and use data","<p class=\"doc-lead\">Playground search, research and crawl runs save their results automatically. Open Datasets to inspect records later and download JSON, JSONL or CSV without collecting or paying for the same sources again.</p><h2 id=\"first-run\">Collect and reopen</h2><p>Create an API key, run a query or public URL in Playground, then select Open saved dataset. Dataset rows preserve source URLs and distinguish search results, crawl pages and research evidence. URL crawls include readable text, titles, descriptions, headings, capture timestamps and source hashes. The default text limits are 50,000 UTF-8 bytes per page and 750,000 bytes per crawl. Check content_truncated and content_error before using a page. Extraction reuses the HTTP capture; it does not execute page JavaScript.</p><p><a data-link href=\"/dashboard/playground\">Open Playground</a> · <a data-link href=\"/dashboard/datasets\">Open Datasets</a></p><h2 id=\"api\">Use the API</h2><p>Read endpoints accept a verified console session or a Bearer API key with mcp:read. Rename and delete require mcp:execute. Every query is scoped to the authenticated account.</p><pre>curl --fail-with-body '{{ORIGIN}}/api/datasets?limit=25' \\\n  -H 'Authorization: Bearer YOUR_API_KEY'\n\ncurl --fail-with-body '{{ORIGIN}}/api/datasets/DATASET_ID/export?format=csv' \\\n  -H 'Authorization: Bearer YOUR_API_KEY' -o results.csv</pre><p>GET /api/datasets/{id}?limit=50&amp;offset=0&amp;q=price returns matching rows and a filtered total. GET /api/datasets/{id}/export exports all rows. PATCH the same ID with {\"name\":\"Saved sources\"} to rename. DELETE removes output while keeping run history and its charge.</p><h2 id=\"cost\">Cost and limits</h2><p>Collection follows the existing wallet prices. Dataset reads and downloads add no collection charge. Saved output is bounded to 2 MB and 1000 records per run. Names have a 120-character limit; page size is at most 100.</p><h2 id=\"failures\">When saving fails</h2><p>Oversized output or a storage error produces dataset:null and a dataset_not_saved warning. The live result remains visible and the collection charge applies. Copy Raw JSON before leaving. There is no automatic retry. Foreign-account IDs return 404; revoked keys return 401; missing scopes return 403.</p><h2 id=\"formats\">Formats and retention</h2><p>JSON and JSONL preserve nested values. CSV puts nested values in JSON cells and prefixes formula-like text for spreadsheet safety. Outputs stay private to the account until explicitly deleted, account deletion or deletion of their linked usage event. Export URLs contain no secrets.</p><h2 id=\"webhooks\">Deliver to an automation</h2><p><a data-link href=\"/docs/dataset-webhooks\">Configure signed dataset notifications</a> to react to new saved outputs without polling.</p>"],"dataset-webhooks":["Dataset webhooks","Data and automation","<p class=\"doc-lead\">Receive a signed notification when a new Playground dataset is saved, then download the output for your automation.</p><h2 id=\"setup\">Set up your receiver</h2><p>Open Datasets → Webhook delivery. Supply a public HTTPS URL on port 443 and save the one-time signing secret in your receiver. Enable delivery, then collect data. Older datasets and runs made while paused do not create events. Read configuration with mcp:read; configure, remove and retry with mcp:execute. A verified console session also works.</p><pre>curl --fail-with-body '{{ORIGIN}}/api/dataset-webhook' \\\n  -X PUT -H 'Authorization: Bearer YOUR_API_KEY' \\\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n  -d '{\"url\":\"https://your-app.example/webhooks/opencrawl\",\"enabled\":true}'</pre><h2 id=\"payload\">Use the saved output</h2><p>The dataset.saved event contains an ID, version 1, creation time, and data with dataset_id, request_id, operation, row_count and a relative API path. It contains no collected page bodies or API credentials. Download /api/datasets/{id}/export?format=jsonl with your own read key after verifying the notification. The signing secret does not authorize downloads.</p><h2 id=\"signature\">Verify before processing</h2><p>Headers: X-OpenCrawl-Event, X-OpenCrawl-Delivery and X-OpenCrawl-Signature. The signature is t=UNIX_SECONDS,v1=HEX_DIGEST. HMAC-SHA256 signs the timestamp, a period, and the exact raw UTF-8 body bytes. Compare in constant time, allow at most five minutes of clock skew, check that the body ID matches the delivery header, and deduplicate that ID durably. Return 2xx after durable acceptance, then process in your own queue.</p><pre>import hashlib\nimport hmac\nimport time\n\n\ndef verify(raw_body: bytes, signature: str, signing_secret: str) -&gt; bool:\n    try:\n        fields = dict(part.split(&quot;=&quot;, 1) for part in signature.split(&quot;,&quot;))\n        timestamp = int(fields[&quot;t&quot;])\n        supplied = fields[&quot;v1&quot;]\n    except (ValueError, KeyError):\n        return False\n    if abs(time.time() - timestamp) &gt; 300:\n        return False\n    expected = hmac.new(signing_secret.encode(),\n                        str(timestamp).encode() + b&quot;.&quot; + raw_body,\n                        hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()\n    return hmac.compare_digest(expected, supplied)\n</pre><h2 id=\"retries\">Retries and history</h2><p>The scheduler requests a production tick every five minutes; timing can be delayed. Each tick claims up to five events with a 60-second lease and a five-second timeout per delivery. 2xx completes delivery. Network/timeouts, transient DNS errors, 408, 429 and 5xx retry up to five attempts. Backoff is at least 60, 300, 1,500 and 3,600 seconds. Other 4xx, redirects and blocked targets fail immediately. No redirects are followed.</p><p>View attempts and outcomes in Datasets → Webhook delivery. Retry failed events with POST /api/dataset-webhook/deliveries/{id}/retry; event ID/body stay the same while the attempt counter resets. Read paginated history at GET /api/dataset-webhook/deliveries?limit=25&amp;offset=0. Foreign IDs return 404; incompatible states return 409. Webhook delivery adds no charge; collection uses the existing wallet rules.</p><h2 id=\"lifecycle\">Pause, rotate and remove</h2><p>PUT enabled:false holds existing events. New runs made while paused do not create events. Rotate with rotate_secret:true; a changed URL automatically rotates the secret. Queued events use current configuration when claimed; in-flight events can finish under the prior configuration. DELETE /api/dataset-webhook removes the endpoint, queued events and history while retaining datasets. Deleting a dataset removes its associated event. Delivery records expire 30 days after creation and are pruned on dispatcher ticks.</p><h2 id=\"deployment\">Deployment and integrations</h2><p>Setup requires encryption configured on this deployment. The protected /api/internal/dataset-webhooks/tick uses the existing scheduler OIDC identity or CRON_SECRET. Preview receives no automatic production ticks. Test with a receiver you control before promotion. For n8n or Make, verify raw bodies and deduplicate in a receiver before passing events into a workflow. This release covers saved Playground outputs; monitor changes and asynchronous job progress remain separate work.</p>"]};
